099801 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186099802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099801 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099801 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099801 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099801 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099801 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099801 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099801 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099801 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "81d7abef-1e5f-5e7e-a713-da16f17f6fd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099802 #edb/ref 1080 "No such $k$ exists" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099802 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099802 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8f2d497f-046e-5ff8-9cc4-36ed95644117" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100618 #edb/ref 1069 "e-267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100618 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value(s) of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-1?$\n![](images/ed/ed102b504e05beeadc9c50229066f14d39ff9f39730f122a40d3597861755486.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100618 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8148cf4c-eae5-54de-adb9-6b8fdf01c7a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100618 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{kx+k} & =\\frac{(x+1)({x}^{2}-x+1)}{k(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x+1)}({x}^{2}-x+1)}{k\\cancel{(x+1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x+1}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can compute the limit at $x=-1,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -1} g(x) = \\lim_\\limits{x\\to -1} \\left(\\dfrac{x^2-x+1}{k}\\right) = \\dfrac{3}{k}.\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}g(x) & =g(-1) \\\\ \\frac{3}{k} & =\\frac{k}{3} \\\\ 9 & ={k}^{2} \\\\ \\pm 3 & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ the value of $k$ must be either $-3$ or $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100619 #edb/ref 1069 "e-268" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100619 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. Find the values of $k$ for which $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$\n![](images/ad/ad328ab7996bbdacac0284ecf282c610bebc3ba7959ddb25dcc78123a5d91384.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100619 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "837cbd93-6e15-537c-8661-37f42294e66f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100619 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $f(x)$, the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x-12}{{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(x+6)}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+6)}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{x+6}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+6}{2+2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{2}^{kx} \\\\  & ={2}^{2k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(2) = 2^{2k}.$\n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ {2}^{2k} & =2 \\\\ {2}^{2k} & ={2}^{1} \\\\ 2k & =1 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\dfrac12$ removes the discontinuity at $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100803 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100803 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100803 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100803 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100803 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100803 #edb/ref 1038 #edb