15 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511432 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511432 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511432 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511432 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511432 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511432 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511432 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511432 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d952cf65-eeb0-5f71-bdd7-74da23f0cdb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511438 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511438 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511438 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511438 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511438 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e5e3e126-997f-5916-8a3a-4894a4d9dc44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511441 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511441 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511441 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511441 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511441 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ec3eded7-8b33-51f5-aa61-b08150e3ba4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511442 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511442 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511442 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511442 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511442 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511442 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511442 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511442 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511442 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ee0499b8-2a39-5262-89e9-bfc3b9bddd29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511461 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511461 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48751" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511461 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511461 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-3?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+14x+33}{2kx+6k}, & x<-3 \\\\ -\\frac{kx}{3}, & x\\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511461 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a4ad86f-33ac-54b8-ac3c-2f4008b70bee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511461 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $g(x)$, the left-sided limit of $g(x)$ at $x=-3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}+14x+33}{2kx+6k} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{(x+11)(x+3)}{2k(x+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{(x+11)\\cancel{(x+3)}}{2k\\cancel{(x+3)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{x+11}{2k} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(-\\frac{kx}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{k(-3)}{3} \\\\  & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $g(-3)=k.$ \n\nSo, for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-3,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}g(x) \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{k} \\\\ {k}^{2} & =4 \\\\ k & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-2$ or $k=2$ remove the discontinuity at $x=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511477 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511477 #edb/ref 1069 "q-339609" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511477 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511477 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value of $b$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=1?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-2}{{x}^{2}-1}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ b, & x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511477 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e2710e1-9e72-5ace-ad58-3320fc10bae0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511477 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-2}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-1)}{(x+1)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-1)}}{(x+1)\\cancel{(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x+2}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x) \\\\ b & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ we need $b=\\frac{3}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511493 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511493 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48747" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511493 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511493 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=2?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}, & x\\ne 2 \\\\ {k}^{2}-3k-12, & x=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511493 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d55624d-78c7-5fb3-b79b-1987319cc6a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511493 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-6x+8} & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-2)}{(x-4)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-2)}}{(x-4)\\cancel{(x-2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x+2}{x-4}=\\frac{2+2}{2-4}=-2.\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x) \\\\ {k}^{2}-3k-12 & =-2 \\\\ {k}^{2}-3k-10 & =0 \\\\ (k+2)(k-5) & =0 \\\\ k & =-2,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=2,$ we need $k=-2$ or $k=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511508 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511508 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511508 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511508 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k>0$ is $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-2?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2}, & x<-2 \\\\ {k}^{1-x}-10, & x\\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511508 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7217df53-39b8-5ccc-a849-6b2d9370fbc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511508 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$ the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-7)(x+2)}{x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-7)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{\\cancel{x+2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}(x-7) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}({k}^{1-x}-10) \\\\  & ={k}^{1-(-2)}-10 \\\\  & ={k}^{3}-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(-2)={k}^{3}-10$ \n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ {k}^{3}-10 & =-9 \\\\ {k}^{3} & =1 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we need $k=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511509 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511509 #edb/ref 1069 "q-339855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511509 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511509 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $f(x)$ continuous at $x=2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15}{x+3}, & x<2 \\\\ 2x+k, & x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511509 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74f22eb1-bd58-519a-94ee-cc4da7917518" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511509 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $f(x)$, the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{15}{x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2+3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(2x+k) \\\\  & =2(2)+k \\\\  & =4+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(2)=4+k.$ \n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ 4+k & =3 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous at $x=2$ for the value $k=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511511 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511511 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511511 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511511 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. Find the value of $a,$ if any, for which $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-2}, & x<2 \\\\ ax, & x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511511 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "791927d1-0973-588f-903c-f13b3a6de1fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511511 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $f(x),$ the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x-2}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}ax=2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-\\infty ,$ there is no value of $a$ that makes $f(x)$ continuous at $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511519 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511519 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48431" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511519 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511519 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=1?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{kx-k}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ k, & x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511519 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "89569c74-5681-5d64-867b-d3414ad5af76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511519 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{kx-k} & =\\frac{(x+3)(x-1)}{k(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)\\cancel{(x-1)}}{k\\cancel{(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x+3}{k}=\\frac{4}{k}.\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x) \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{k} \\\\ {k}^{2} & =4 \\\\ k & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ we need $k=\\pm 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511525 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511525 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511525 #edb/ref 1069 "q-303064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511525 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511525 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=k?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}-8}{x-2}, & x<k \\\\ -2k-x, & x\\ge k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nArranged in **ascending order**,\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,k=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511525 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8cbd258b-287d-5f95-b759-449d64d2387b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511525 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $g(x)$, the left-sided limit of $g(x)$ at $x=k$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{3}-8}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)({x}^{2}+2x+4)}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}({x}^{2}+2x+4)}{\\cancel{x-2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}({x}^{2}+2x+4) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+2k+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {k}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{+}}(-2k-x) \\\\  & =-2k-k \\\\  & =-3k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $g(k)=-3k.$ \n\nSo, for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=k,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ {k}^{2}+2k+4 & =-3k \\\\ {k}^{2}+5k+4 & =0 \\\\ (k+4)(k+1) & =0 \\\\ k & =-4,\\,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous at $x=k$ for the values\n\n$$\nk=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,k=-1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511530 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511530 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48328" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511530 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511530 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\nh(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{k}^{2}}{x-k}, & x\\ne k \\\\ -4, & x=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis continuous at $x=k$, find the value of the constant $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511530 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99920a9c-2e7f-5c68-986e-e57bd85cf9e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511530 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and cancel any common factors: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{k}^{2}}{x-k} & =\\frac{(x+k)(x-k)}{(x-k)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+k)\\cancel{(x-k)}}{\\cancel{x-k}} \\\\  & =x+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=k,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to k}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to k}(x+k)=k+k=2k.\n$$\n\nFor $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=k,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(k) & ={lim}_{x\\to k}h(x) \\\\ -4 & =2k \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=k,$ we need $k=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
